{
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  // Deploying: `npm run build` makes @astrojs/cloudflare emit a fully
  // resolved dist/server/wrangler.json from THIS file (it injects
  // main: entry.mjs + assets: ../client — do not add main/assets here).
  // Deploy with `npm run deploy`, or in Cloudflare Workers Builds set:
  //   root dir `site` · build `npm run build`
  //   deploy `npx wrangler deploy --config dist/server/wrangler.json`
  // Must stay byte-identical to the Worker that Workers Builds is connected to
  // (`texashomeintelligence`, no hyphens, repo zmpersonal/texashomeintelligence).
  // It was previously the hyphenated `texas-home-intelligence`, which Cloudflare
  // flagged as a mismatch; a `wrangler deploy` under that name would have
  // created a SECOND Worker on its own workers.dev hostname rather than
  // updating staging. Do not "tidy" this into the hyphenated form to match the
  // D1/KV resource names below — those are separate resources and are correctly
  // hyphenated. Renaming the Worker is an owner action in the dashboard first,
  // this field second.
  "name": "texashomeintelligence",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-04-15",
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat"],

  // Seam 3 (HANDOFF.md). These IDs were long commented as "LOCAL PLACEHOLDERS
  // ONLY"; they are not. All three were verified against the owner's real
  // Cloudflare account — the two KV namespace IDs match the live namespaces
  // (texas-home-intelligence-projects and texashomeintelligence-session), and
  // the D1 id matches the live texas-home-intelligence-db. Nothing needs
  // swapping before a deploy.
  "kv_namespaces": [
    // Intake project state and resumable return tokens (src/lib/kv.ts writes
    // `project:` and `token:` keys here).
    { "binding": "PROJECTS_KV", "id": "d5c64f949583450db1bd1cee073caf23" },
    // Astro Sessions. No application code touches this binding; the
    // @astrojs/cloudflare adapter claims it by name at build time ("Enabling
    // sessions with Cloudflare KV with the SESSION KV binding"), so the name
    // must stay exactly `SESSION`.
    {
      "binding": "SESSION",
      "id": "cc7a5a1c29e04852ace9723ed69969bb"
    }
  ],
  // Durable store for intake projects, answers, generated briefs, accounts,
  // home profiles, reminders and the weekly-email tables (src/lib/db.ts,
  // src/lib/account/db.ts).
  //
  // MIGRATION STATE, VERIFIED 2026-09-04 — INFERRED, NOT RECORDED.
  // All four migrations are applied to the REMOTE database:
  //   0001_init.sql                    0002_dashboard_launch_signups.sql
  //   0003_accounts_home_reminders.sql 0004_weekly_email.sql
  // HOW THAT WAS ESTABLISHED, and the caveat that matters: by READ-ONLY SCHEMA
  // INSPECTION of the remote database (`sqlite_master`), not by reading a
  // ledger. All 22 objects those four files create — 18 tables and 4 indexes —
  // are present, and the column-level DDL of the 0003 and 0004 tables matches
  // the migration files exactly (every column, type, default and CHECK).
  // So this is an INFERENCE FROM OBJECT PRESENCE. It cannot distinguish "the
  // migration ran" from "something else created objects of the same shape", and
  // it carries NO APPLIED TIMESTAMPS, because none are recorded anywhere.
  // The previous note here claimed only 0001 was applied, on 2026-08-29. That
  // was correct when written and went stale across three migrations without
  // anything noticing — which is the cost of having no ledger, stated plainly.
  //
  // THERE IS STILL NO MIGRATION LEDGER — no `d1_migrations` table exists on the
  // remote database (confirmed absent, same read). Round 13 did the two things
  // that have to happen BEFORE one can safely be created; creating it is a
  // write to production and remains the owner's to run. HANDOFF.md carries the
  // exact command.
  //
  // ⚠️ THE HAZARD ROUND 13 REMOVED, recorded so nobody reintroduces it.
  // `wrangler d1 migrations apply` globs `**/*.sql` under `migrations_dir`, so
  // EVERY .sql file in that directory is a migration it will run. `seed.sql`
  // used to live there, and it inserts sample rows (fake names, example.com
  // addresses). With an empty ledger, the first `--remote` run would have
  // executed those INSERTs against production. Verified before the move by
  // running `wrangler d1 migrations list --local` against a throwaway database,
  // which printed seed.sql alongside 0001-0004 as unapplied.
  // It now lives at `site/fixtures/seed.sql`, outside `migrations_dir`, and the
  // same command lists only 0001-0004. THE RULE THAT KEEPS THIS TRUE:
  // `migrations/` contains exactly the files wrangler may run against
  // production, and nothing else. Never put a fixture, a backfill or a one-off
  // script there.
  "d1_databases": [
    {
      "binding": "DB",
      "database_name": "texas-home-intelligence-db",
      "database_id": "0b1f11b2-b8f4-4eeb-b36e-4debd9f5c956",
      // Both values are wrangler's OWN DEFAULTS, quoted from its config schema:
      // migrations_dir "defaults to './migrations'", migrations_table "defaults
      // to 'd1_migrations'". Stating them changes no behaviour — it makes the
      // directory whose contents get run against production explicit at the
      // point someone would edit it, rather than implicit in a tool default.
      "migrations_dir": "migrations",
      "migrations_table": "d1_migrations"
    }
  ]
}
